    {'heading': 'Lesson XXXIX'},
    {'para': '**414. Verbs. Root-class, cont’d. Roots ending in consonants.** The endings of the 2nd and 3rd sing. impf. act. are generally dropped, and the resulting root-final treated according to the usual rules for finals. Cf. §§ 239, 242. But a root ending in a dental mute sometimes drops this final mute instead of the added ⟨s⟩ in the second person; and, on the other hand, a root or stem ending in ⟨s⟩ sometimes drops this ⟨s⟩ instead of the added ⟨t⟩ in the third person: in either case establishing the ordinary relation of ⟨s⟩ and ⟨t⟩ in the second and third persons.'},
    {'para': '**415.** Roots in ⟨c⟩ and ⟨j⟩ substitute ⟨k⟩ for those letters before ⟨t⟩, ⟨th⟩ and ⟨s⟩ (which then becomes ⟨ṣ⟩); and ⟨g⟩ before ⟨dh⟩. Thus, ⟨vac⟩ ‘speak’: ⟨vacmi⟩, ⟨vakṣi⟩, ⟨vakti⟩ (only these three forms used).'},
    {'para': '**416.** Root 1⟨vid⟩ ‘know’ (act. only):'},
